  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Monday)\nIssued at 1139 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nWe've had a windy start to the day with sustained winds 15-25 mph\nand gusts up to 35 mph as the mid-level pressure gradient\ncontinues to tighten across the Mid-South. Ahead of a Marginal\nRisk for strong to severe thunderstorms later this afternoon,\nwe're looking at mostly cloudy skies, temperatures in the 70s,\nand dewpoint temperatures in the 60s. A mid-level low pressure\nsystem, currently centered over eastern Kansas, continues to\nchurn closer to the Mississippi Valley. Ahead of it, an elongated\ncold front, extending north to south bisecting the CONUS,\ncontinues to push further east. As this front pushes further\neast, into our area, a line of thunderstorms will begin impacting\nthe Mid-South. Digging into the parameter spacing, forecast\nsoundings are hinting at around 300-500 J/kg of SBCAPE, around\n400 J/kg of DCAPE, around 25-30 kts of effective shear, and mid\nand low level lapse rates <6.0 C/km. If this messy line of\nthunderstorms can overcome very meager instability and persistent\ncloud cover, the primary severe threat will be damaging winds. A\nsecondary threat of a quick spin up tornado or two also exists\namidst looping hodographs with updraft helicity values generally\nless than 100 m2/s2.\n\nAs this line crosses the Mississippi River, instability begins to\nwane slightly as CAPE fields become further weakened and the sun\nbegins to set. Localized flash flooding will become more of a\nthreat heading into the evening hours with PWAT values around 1.6-\n1.8\", around the 99th percentile for this time of the year. Most\nof our heavy rainfall will fall within a 12 hour span with\nforecast amounts generally between 1-2\". Locally higher amounts\ncannot be ruled out as latest NBM probabilities have a 50-60%\nchance of exceeding 2\" across much of the Mid-South. In the event\nthat flash flooding does occur, we ask you to never enter flooded\nroadways with a good rule of thumb to turn around and don't\ndrown. This line of storms is expected to clear our forecast area\nby sunrise tomorrow as the aforementioned cold front pushes east\ninto Middle Tennessee.\n\nDrier and cooler air will begin to quickly filter in behind this\nfront as cool surface high pressure sets in. Highs will be\ngenerally in the lower 70s tomorrow and Thursday with lows in the\n40s Thursday morning. By the end of the workweek, a potent upper-\nlevel shortwave will begin to push into the Mississippi Valley\nwith an attendant reinforcing cold front. A cool, dry airmass\nwill be associated with this secondary front resulting in little\nto no precipitation. Instead, much cooler temperatures will\nfollow behind it. Some early morning frost is anticipated\nSaturday morning, mainly over West Tennessee and across much of\nthe Mid-South Sunday morning. As far as high temperatures go this\nweekend, we're looking at readings in the upper 50s to lower 60s.\nEnjoy the cooler air while it lasts as the latest 8-14 day CPC\noutlook has us leaning (40-50% chance) above normal heading into\nmid-November.\n\nAEH",
